  body: "# Good reason and differentiated evidential burdens in legacy JSA\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\
    \nThe captured sanctions guidance leaves good reason undefined and requires all relevant circumstances,
    while distinguishing the burden of establishing a sanctionable failure from the claimant’s explanation.\n\
    \n## Scope and exceptions\n\nDMG 34201 identifies the different intermediate-sanction context; DMG
    34202 distinguishes misconduct, where facts and evidence can still be addressed. The allocation in
    DMG 34205 must be read with those exceptions. This is not a universal burden for all decisions, nor
    a current UC or New Style JSA good-reason rule.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG 34001, PDF
    page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/628f5e0bd3bf7f0373c74ef9/dmgch34.pdf#page=2)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 34: Sanctions.\n- [DMG 34201, PDF page 58](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/628f5e0bd3bf7f0373c74ef9/dmgch34.pdf#page=58)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 34: Sanctions.\n- [DMG 34202, PDF page 58](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/628f5e0bd3bf7f0373c74ef9/dmgch34.pdf#page=58)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 34: Sanctions.\n- [DMG 34203, PDF page 58](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/628f5e0bd3bf7f0373c74ef9/dmgch34.pdf#page=58)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 34: Sanctions.\n- [DMG 34205, PDF page 58](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/628f5e0bd3bf7f0373c74ef9/dmgch34.pdf#page=58)
    — DMG Vol 6 Ch 34: Sanctions.\n- [Inspected continuation, PDF page 9](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/628f5e0bd3bf7f0373c74ef9/dmgch34.pdf#page=9)
    — context for the selected paragraph.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation
    of selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required.
    Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\
    \nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice,
    an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes
    and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is
    claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol6-ch34`: PDF SHA-256 `0cf245521764b405f4d8983f1b93f131dbe6d9cacd000a0ed9edeb1aca4f9b0b`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `b33b6233590a7183795624a56525dfb2594dae0d201c5d33b36ff7ec8a3c74ce`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-burden-of-proof
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The passage distinguishes the Secretary of State’s failure burden from the claimant’s
      good-reason onus and expressly qualifies misconduct.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol6-ch34/0058
      quote: "34205 The onus is on the Secretary of State to show there is a sanctionable failure (also
        see further\nguidance at DMG 34033). Except in misconduct cases, once the Secretary of State has
        shown a\nsanctionable failure has occurred, it is the claimant’s burden of proof to show good
        reason for that failure\n(see DMG 34207).\n\n\nNote: For full guidance on Misconduct see DMG 34531
        et seq."
      locator: DMG 34205
  - target: term/legacy-jsa-prior-information
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The chapter’s prior-information requirement and meaningful representations are part
      of the surrounding process; they are not replaced by asking for good reason after any failure.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol6-ch34/0008
      quote: "34033 The ‘prior information requirement’ principle is from a judgement of the Court of
        Appeal 1. The\njudgement provided, in the general interest of fairness,\n\n\n1.   each claimant’s
        responsibilities and\n\n\n2. the consequences of not meeting them\n\n\nshould be set out clearly
        in understandable terms at the stage of specification of particular actions or\nactivities so
        that claimants can make informed and meaningful representations at the relevant time.\n\n\nNote
        1: The ‘prior information requirement’ may therefore be relevant both to whether the claimant
        has\nbeen validly referred to a specific requirement and also to whether there was good reason
        for not\ncomplying with it."
      locator: DMG 34033
    - page: page/dmg-vol6-ch34/0009
      quote: "34035 The ‘prior information requirement’ should be approached on an individual case by
        case basis.\nThe ability of claimants to access information and express themselves will vary considerably
        in their\nlevels of education and ability to understand the complexities of the sanctions regime
        at a time when\n\n\n1. they may be under considerable stress and\n\n\n2. the outcome of which
        any failure to comply may have serious consequences on their ability to meet\ntheir living needs.\n\
        \n\nIn the interests of fairness, therefore, they should be in no doubt of what is expected of
        them, the\nconsequences of failing to comply with any requirement and be able to make meaningful\n\
        representations to a DM before a decision is made. Also see the guidance on ‘regularity’ at DMG
        34039."
      locator: DMG 34035
